WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.570
Everything I do comes from seeing patients

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:10.420
and not having an answer
for how to help them.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.670
Right now, all of us are
using mobile technologies

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.170
and computers for typing and texting,

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.740
and sending messages, generating language.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.670
Folks with locked-in syndrome

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.323
don't have any way to do that!

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:26.500
Gregory had brain damage.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.630
I don't think he'll
ever be able to do this.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.630
And Dr. Melanie said, "It
is rewiring his brain.

00:00:34.107 --> 00:00:36.230
"He'll be able to do it."

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.010
It was astounding to me.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.520
It will be real fun to
see where this goes.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.293
It's almost science fiction.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.430
When the phone rang at eight
o'clock in the morning,

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.630
and it was the hospital and they told me

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.800
I needed to get there right away.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.890
They found out it was

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:07.200
a brain stem major stroke.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.393
Gregory lost his speech.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.130
He lost the use of his body limbs.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.403
He was instantly paralyzed
and his speech was taken.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.830
And we were given that it
was locked-in syndrome.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.570
And I had never heard of it,
didn't know anything about it,

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.530
and I remember leaving
the hospital that day

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.930
not understanding at all but thinking

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.730
that Greg's body was dead
but his brain was alive,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.200
and how horrific that was.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.850
I am 100% dependent on my caregivers.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.070
I am bathed, I have range of motion,

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.400
I am dressed, I have to have
finished with my breakfast

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.260
and wait two hours
before I can be laid down

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.830
for range of motion or
any physical movement.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.550
I'm physically put into my wheelchair

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.760
with a Hoyer lift and my caregiver's help.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.540
I am a tube feeder; no liquid
or food enters my mouth.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.910
I would be at risk of
getting aspiration pneumonia.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.550
It frustrates me when people ignore me.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.640
I may have something to say

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.430
and they will not look
me in the face or ask me

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.310
if I have something I want to say.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.170
It frustrates me when
people act like I am deaf.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.950
They talk around me to
each other but not to me.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.360
They don't include me
in on the conversations.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.600
It frustrates me when people
ask the people around me

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.413
questions that I can answer,
as if I weren't there.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.930
My name is Dr. Melanie Fried-Oken.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.610
I am a professor at Oregon
Health &amp; Science University

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:41.850
in Portland, Oregon.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.190
So we do have a full
agenda for the hour today,

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.470
mostly something from every team,

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.470
so let's start with software
and look at Pivotal Tracker.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.920
Our team is unique.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.050
We have four different teams that together

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.100
have worked for 12 years to develop

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.803
our noninvasive brain-computer interface.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.020
I am a speech-language pathologist.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.890
I work primarily on our brain-computer

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.880
interface research projects.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.800
Our brain-computer interface
technology is primarily

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.870
aimed at people with
very severe disabilities.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.690
Locked-in syndrome is a term that we use

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:21.790
to describe someone who is essentially

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.253
locked into his or her body.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.170
The kind of classic form
of locked-in syndrome

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.490
involves only eye movement or blinking.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.720
But there's also complete
LIS, where the person has lost

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.920
all of their voluntary motor function.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:41.150
So even the ability to blink
or to move the eyes is lost.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.820
If you have no voluntary motor function,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.970
you have no way to communicate
with another human being.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:49.080
So that's the gap that
we're trying to fill.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.890
A lot of the brain-computer interface work

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:56.200
is being done in engineering
labs or neuroscience labs

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.520
with graduate students and researchers,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.640
and has not been tried.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.400
We take our work out into the community

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.350
and we work with many individuals
with severe disabilities.

00:04:14.615 --> 00:04:15.643
Hey there, Greg!

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:17.503
Nice to see ya!

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.517
Doin' well today?

00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:23.170
He says, "Yes."
Good,

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:24.720
good to hear it.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.950
Brain-computer interface
allows someone to control

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.480
a computer just using
only their brainwaves.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.750
In our system, we have an electrode cap

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.900
that holds electrodes against the scalp,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.750
and they can watch what the
system is presenting to them.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.530
Lift his head up a little bit

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.763
so that I can slide the cap on.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.330
Okay, are you ready to go?

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.980
Okay, so it'll be, "This is fun."

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.633
Here we go.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.510
There's one letter presented at a time

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.720
in the middle of the screen.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.230
So it's just flash, flash,
flash, letter, letter, letter.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.030
You're watching that
stream of letters go by,

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.560
and when you see the letter you want,

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.410
you have that involuntary brain

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.317
response of, "Yes, that one."

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.580
Instead of someone moving

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.620
their hand to press the letter M,

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.040
they're looking at a screen,

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.340
and when they see the letter M,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.890
they get a special brainwave,
a surprise brainwave,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.650
that's called the P300.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:30.633
And that P300 brainwave is
being taken as their keystroke.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.130
I have tested various programs created

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.200
by the engineers of
computer programmers to see

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.820
if they are computer-friendly
for people like me.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.300
The exercises I do on the
computer are challenging,

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.913
and I like being challenged.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:02.300
All right, nice job!

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.150
That was awesome!

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.870
The kind of general wisdom
in the BCI field right now

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.690
is that it typically
works for healthy users

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.900
under controlled situations, you know?

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.360
Our BCI keyboard works reliably

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.710
for most healthy people who try it.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:23.630
But when we take it out into the field,

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.153
it's less likely to work.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.323
Post One, American
Legion, Portland, Oregon.

00:06:36.554 --> 00:06:40.273
And the Gregonator, Greg
Bieker out there tonight, yeah!

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.810
The pie in the sky, the dream goal

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.620
for brain-computer interface
would be to allow people

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.510
with severe disabilities and
communication impairments

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.660
to communicate in real time.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.130
That's really not possible
with any kind of assistive

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.030
technology for
communication at this point.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:00.870
Obviously, we wanna make it faster,

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:02.550
make it better; that's
what we're working towards.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.950
But the first goal is to
make it work consistently

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.950
and reliably for the people who need it.

00:07:09.653 --> 00:07:13.270
♪ God bless the USA ♪

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.570
I have had people be
fearful to approach me.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.980
I would want them to know
that I'm not contagious.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.680
I would want them to know it's
okay to approach me and talk.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:26.130
I would want them to know
I have a sense of humor.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.070
I would want people to
never have to experience

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.370
what I go through daily.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.543
Locked-in syndrome is not for sissies!

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:35.720
I am excited to be on board for whatever

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:38.090
this new technology takes us.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:39.740
I'm happy to be part of the team.

